window.docContents[82].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Functors___-Applicative-Functors___-and-Monads--Summary--Applicative-Functors":{"contents":"An applicative functor is a functor with two additional operations:\n\n* , which is the same operator as that for * , which allows a function to be applied in the context of the functor.\n\nWhile monads can represent arbitrary programs with control flow, applicative functors can only run function arguments from left to right.\nBecause they are less powerful, they provide less control to programs written against the interface, while the implementor of the method has a greater degree of freedom.\nSome useful types can implement  but not .\n\nIn fact, the type classes , , and  form a hierarchy of power.\nMoving up the hierarchy, from  towards , allows more powerful programs to be written, but fewer types implement the more powerful classes.\nPolymorphic programs should be written to use as weak of an abstraction as possible, while datatypes should be given instances that are as powerful as possible.\nThis maximizes code re-use.\nThe more powerful type classes extend the less powerful ones, which means that an implementation of  provides implementations of  and  for free.\n\nEach class has a set of methods to be implemented and a corresponding contract that specifies additional rules for the methods.\nPrograms that are written against these interfaces expect that the additional rules are followed, and may be buggy if they are not.\nThe default implementations of 's methods in terms of 's, and of 's in terms of 's, will obey these rules.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Summary","header":"5.7.3. Applicative Functors","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Functors___-Applicative-Functors___-and-Monads--Summary--Applicative-Functors"},"/#http-request-ex":{"contents":"An HTTP request begins with an identification of a HTTP method, such as GET or POST, along with a URI and an HTTP version.\nDefine an inductive type that represents an interesting subset of the HTTP methods, and a structure that represents HTTP responses.\nResponses should have a  instance that makes it possible to debug them.\nUse a type class to associate different  actions with each HTTP method, and write a test harness as an  action that calls each method and prints the result.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Positive Numbers\u0009Exercises","header":"3.1.6.3. HTTP Requests","id":"/#http-request-ex"},"/#inner-insertion-sort-loop":{"contents":"The inner loop of insertion sort can be implemented as a tail-recursive function that takes the array and the index of the element being inserted as arguments.\nThe element being inserted is repeatedly swapped with the element to its left until either the element to the left is smaller or the beginning of the array is reached.\nThe inner loop is structurally recursive on the  that is inside the  used to index into the array:\n\n\n\nIf the index  is , then the element being inserted into the sorted region has reached the beginning of the region and is the smallest.\nIf the index is , then the element at  should be compared to the element at .\nNote that while  is a ,  is just a  because it results from the  field of .\nNonetheless, the proof automation used for checking array index notation includes a solver for linear integer arithmetic, so  is automatically usable as an index.\n\nThe two elements are looked up and compared.\nIf the element to the left is less than or equal to the element being inserted, then the loop is finished and the invariant has been restored.\nIf the element to the left is greater than the element being inserted, then the elements are swapped and the inner loop begins again.\n takes both of its indices as s, using the same tactics as array indexing behind the scenes to ensure that they are in bounds.\n\nNonetheless, the  used for the recursive call needs a proof that  is in bounds for the result of swapping two elements.\nThe  tactic's database contains the fact that swapping two elements of an array doesn't change its size.\nCombining this with the fact that  is in bounds for the original array,  can conclude that  is in bounds after the swap.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009Insertion Sort and Array Mutation","header":"8.6.1. The Inner Loop","id":"/#inner-insertion-sort-loop"}});